{
  "slug": "401k-auto-enrollment-final-rules",
  "title": "401(k) Auto-Enrollment Final Rules: 3% Default, 10% Step",
  "metaDescription": "401(k) automatic enrollment rules start covered plans at 3% to 10% and raise it annually to at least 10%. You can opt out or change your rate at any time.",
  "h1": "New 401(k) Auto-Enrollment Rules Start Workers at 3%",
  "cardBlurb": "Learn how the final 401(k) automatic enrollment rules affect your paycheck, how default contributions increase, and how to opt out.",
  "introText": "Covered new 401(k) and 403(b) retirement plans must automatically enroll eligible employees at a contribution rate of at least 3%, increasing each year until the rate reaches at least 10%, but you can opt out or set a different contribution percentage. The [U.S. Department of the Treasury](https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0690) and the [Internal Revenue Service (IRS)](https://www.irs.gov/) issued final regulations implementing this SECURE 2.0 Act provision on November 17, 2026. Treasury estimates the provision will increase retirement savings by an average of $4.2 billion annually over the 2023 to 2032 period.\n\nThe statutory mandate under [26 U.S. Code 414A](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/414A) sets an initial uniform contribution rate between 3% and 10% of pay, followed by annual automatic increases of 1 percentage point up to at least 10% and at most 15%. However, plans established before December 29, 2022, businesses under three years old, small employers with 10 or fewer workers, church plans, governmental plans, and Savings Incentive Match Plan for Employees (SIMPLE) 401(k) plans are exempt from the federal requirement. Employees always retain the legal right to opt out entirely or choose their own savings rate.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "What the Final Auto-Enrollment Rules Require",
      "body": "Covered 401(k) and 403(b) plans must automatically enroll eligible employees at a default rate of at least 3% of compensation. The statutory framework established by Congress in [26 U.S. Code 414A](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/414A) requires the initial uniform percentage to be not less than 3% and not more than 10% during the employee's first year of participation. After this initial participation period, the plan must automatically increase the employee's contribution rate by 1 percentage point at the start of each subsequent plan year. These annual 1-point increases must continue until the default contribution rate reaches at least 10%, subject to a statutory maximum of 15%.\n\nTwo distinct federal effective dates apply to these automatic enrollment rules. Under Public Law 117-328 (Division T, Title I, Section 101(c)), the statutory amendments apply to plan years beginning after December 31, 2024. In press release SB-0690, the [U.S. Department of the Treasury](https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0690) stated that its final regulations generally apply to plan years beginning after December 31, 2027, giving employers time to make necessary administrative changes. The checked primary sources do not state which date governs a specific workplace plan, and workers should not assume an existing plan is exempt or has until 2028 without reviewing formal plan notices.\n\nEmployees who are automatically enrolled retain the absolute right to opt out or adjust their contribution amount. Under 26 U.S.C. 414A(b)(3), an employee can elect not to have any salary-reduction contributions made or elect to contribute at a different percentage entirely. When an employee does not select specific investment options for their automatic contributions, the statute requires the plan to invest those amounts in accordance with federal default-investment regulations under 29 CFR 2550.404c-5."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Which Plans Are Exempt",
      "body": "Several categories of retirement plans are completely exempt from the mandatory automatic enrollment rules under [26 U.S. Code 414A(c)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/414A). The federal statute specifically excludes plans established before December 29, 2022, which was the enactment date of Public Law 117-328. This grandfathering provision applies to any cash or deferred arrangement under section 401(k) or annuity contract under section 403(b) in existence prior to that date.\n\nThe law also exempts distinct employer and plan categories:\n\n- Small employers that have not normally employed more than 10 employees, remaining exempt until one year after the close of the first taxable year in which the business normally employed more than 10 workers.\n- New businesses that have been in existence, together with any predecessor company, for less than 3 years.\n- Governmental plans maintained by federal, state, or local government entities.\n- Church plans established for church employees.\n- SIMPLE 401(k) plans established under section 401(k)(11) of the Internal Revenue Code.\n\nThe [U.S. Department of the Treasury](https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0690) stated that the final regulations clarify these statutory exceptions for qualifying small and new businesses, governmental plans, church plans, and SIMPLE 401(k) plans. If an employer fits into an exempt category, it is not required by federal law to implement automatic enrollment. However, an exempt employer may still choose to offer voluntary automatic enrollment under its own plan terms."
    },
    {
      "heading": "How the Default Rate Rises Year by Year",
      "body": "Federal law outlines a clear progression for automatic contribution increases over an employee's career. The minimum path under the rule, shown as our illustrative arithmetic, starts at a 3% contribution rate in year 1 and increases by 1 percentage point for each completed year of participation until reaching 10% after seven step increases.\n\n| Participation year | Default employee contribution rate | Annual step change |\n| :--- | ---: | :--- |\n| Year 1 | 3% | Initial required minimum |\n| Year 2 | 4% | Up 1 percentage point |\n| Year 3 | 5% | Up 1 percentage point |\n| Year 4 | 6% | Up 1 percentage point |\n| Year 5 | 7% | Up 1 percentage point |\n| Year 6 | 8% | Up 1 percentage point |\n| Year 7 | 9% | Up 1 percentage point |\n| Year 8 | 10% | Target minimum reached (seven steps) |\n\nA covered plan is permitted to set a higher starting rate up to 10% in the first year, or implement faster annual increases up to a maximum statutory cap of 15%. To see how this baseline schedule affects gross compensation, consider a worker earning a constant $60,000 annual salary. This is pre-tax, salary-only illustrative arithmetic and not a prediction of investment returns, employer contributions, or take-home pay.\n\nAt a $60,000 salary, a 3% default contribution in Year 1 equals $1,800 per year, or $150 per month. By Year 8, when the automatic escalation reaches the 10% minimum target, an employee earning the same $60,000 salary would contribute $6,000 per year, or $500 per month. The statutory escalator increases savings gradually, but you can alter or stop this schedule at any time."
    },
    {
      "heading": "How to Opt Out or Change Your Rate",
      "body": "Automatic enrollment is never mandatory for the individual employee. Under [26 U.S. Code 414A(b)(3)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/26/414A), every participant has the affirmative right to opt out of contributions entirely or select an alternative contribution percentage that differs from the plan default. You can set your rate to 0%, pick an intermediate rate like 5%, or elect to contribute up to the annual legal ceiling.\n\nEmployers must provide participants with formal notices explaining automatic enrollment, default contribution percentages, and opt-out procedures. The [U.S. Department of the Treasury](https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0690) noted that the final regulations simplify plan communications by allowing certain required notices to be combined. The rules also reduce annual notice requirements for qualifying employees who choose not to participate, provided those workers receive annual reminders and any plan documents they are entitled to request.\n\nWhen you receive an automatic enrollment notice from your employer, examine the details carefully. Check the specified default contribution percentage, the date automatic payroll deductions begin, the annual escalation schedule, and the exact steps required to submit an opt-out election. If you do not want deductions taken from your paycheck, submit your election before the plan's stated deadline."
    },
    {
      "heading": "What the Final Regulations Changed for Employers",
      "body": "The final Treasury regulations addressed key operational considerations for employers establishing or operating automatic contribution arrangements. First, the [U.S. Department of the Treasury](https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0690) stated that the final rules give employers administrative flexibility in the timing and amount of automatic contribution increases within statutory boundaries. This allows plan sponsors to align automatic escalation dates with payroll calendars or merit review cycles while remaining compliant with the statutory 1-point minimum.\n\nSecond, the final regulations simplify communication burdens for plan administrators. By permitting the combination of certain required participant notices and eliminating redundant annual notices for confirmed non-participants who receive annual reminders, the rules aim to reduce administrative costs. Finally, the regulations explain how automatic enrollment requirements apply following corporate mergers, clarifying how combined entities must navigate plan differences without disrupting existing employee savings arrangements."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Our Takeaway",
      "body": "For workers at a covered new 401(k) or 403(b) plan: expect enrollment at 3% or more (first-year range 3% to 10%), raised 1 point a year to at least 10% (max 15%), and you can opt out or choose another rate; check your plan notice for whether the plan is exempt or voluntarily auto-enrolls with another default. Both dates (the statute applies to plan years beginning after December 31, 2024, while Treasury says final regulations generally apply to plan years beginning after December 31, 2027) are stated with official attribution, and primary sources do not say which governs a specific plan. If your plan automatically enrolls you, check your plan notice for the exact deduction date, compare your deferrals against the [2027 401(k) contribution limits](/guides/retirement-contribution-limits-2027/), and use the [401(k) calculator](/retirement/401k-calculator/) to test how different contribution rates affect your take-home pay."
    }
  ],
